    "id": "13",
    "title": "Hildefonsus Composes Mary's Miracles",
    "incipit": "ተብህለ፡ ከመ፡ ሀሎ፡ በውስተ፡ ቤተ፡ ክርስቲያን፡ ዘጥልጥልያ፡ ኤጲስ፡ ቆጶስ፡ ዘስሙ፡ ደቅስዮስ፡ ወኮነ፡ ብእሴ፡ ቡሩከ፡ ወኄረ፡ ወፈራሄ፡ እግዚአብሔር",
    "manuscript_count": 360,
    "translations": [
      {
        "author": "Wallis Budge",
        "text": "<p>Now there was a certain bishop in the church of Teltelya [Toledo, Spain] whose name was Dexius [Hildephonsus]. He was a righteous man who feared God and loved Our Lady Mary exceedingly.</p> <br> <p>Dexius’s mouth declared her praise, and his heart was filled with thoughts of her. He ministered to her with exceedingly great diligence and care, and he wrote the book of her miracles and history, never ceasing to meditate upon them every evening and morning.</p> <br> <p>It came to pass that when he had nearly finished writing of her praises, Our Lady Mary, the Majesty of Christian people, brought him glad tidings and said to him, “Give me the book of my history and miracles, which my beloved Son has made.”</p> <br> <p>She took the book of her miracles and, opening it, began to read. She was exceedingly pleased with the book.</p> <br> <p>Mary said to Dexius: “Behold, my beloved, how you have toiled for me. Behold now that I am very pleased with you, and that I give you thanks. The blessing of my beloved Son shall be upon you and shall dwell in your house. Because you have written this book for me, he shall bless your children and those who descend from you with the blessing of heaven.”</p> <br> <p>Having said these words, she disappeared from him.</p> <br> <p>The bishop rejoiced with an exceeding great joy because Mary had brought him glad tidings. She had shown herself to him openly, and she had spoken to him directly. He loved her even more [than before], and his heart was smitten with deep love for her.</p> <br> <p>Then he meditated in his heart and said, “Behold, I will exalt her honor and majesty, and I will do so with more zeal than ever.”</p> <br> <p>He organized a great festival, the like of which man had never made, in commemoration of Mary.</p> <br> <p>The festival fell not on the day of her glorious Nativity, but before it. He inaugurated the celebration of the festival on this day, and he established regulations for it.</p> <br> <p>To this day, all the people of the city celebrate the festival according to the ordinance of the bishop, and they keep the feast of the commemoration of Mary with joy and gladness.</p> <br> <p>Note: This English translation was composed by Wallis Budge in the early 1900s but updated by the PEMM team for modern readers. We have replaced old-fashioned terms (e.g., thee, thou), stripped old-fashioned word endings (e.g., striketh), and corrected offensive language (e.g., lame, barren). However, Budge made some of these translations from a peculiar manuscript, which often had missing words and sentences, so the translations don't always do justice to the story in its original, earliest form. We hope to retranslate the story from better manuscripts in future. His translations are from what he calls MS A, which is PEMM MS B-Oslo (SCOL) 248; MS B, which is PEMM MS B-Dublin (CBL) 914; and Brit. Mus. Orient. No. 652, which is PEMM MS BOr (BL) 652.</p>"
      }
    ]
