        "text": "<p>There lived in the desert of Scete many holy fathers, and a countless number of them died there.</p> <br> <p>There also lived there many saints who celebrated their commemorations, and who put their trust in their prayers.</p> <br> <p>One night, when they gathered in the church at the hour of prayer and were making supplication to Our Lady Mary, behold—a great light appeared.</p> <br> <p>The sainted ones who had died there came, and with them were the Holy Fathers <i>Abba</i> Macarius, <i>Abba</i> John, <i>Abba</i> Muse, and <i>Abba</i> John Kama.</p> <br> <p>They were arrayed in robes of light, and crowns of the Kingdom were on their heads.</p> <br> <p>They were chanting sweet songs of the spirit, and after this they went into the sanctuary of the church.</p> <br> <p>When the holy men saw this sight, they rejoiced with great joy. They glorified the God who is most glorious, and they sang praises to Our Lady Mary.</p> <br> <p>Note: This English translation was composed by Wallis Budge in the early 1900s but updated by the PEMM team for modern readers. We have replaced old-fashioned terms (e.g., thee, thou), stripped old-fashioned word endings (e.g., striketh), and corrected offensive language (e.g., lame, barren). However, Budge made some of these translations from a peculiar manuscript, which often had missing words and sentences, so the translations don't always do justice to the story in its original, earliest form. We hope to retranslate the story from better manuscripts in future. His translations are from what he calls MS A, which is PEMM MS B-Oslo (SCOL) 248; MS B, which is PEMM MS B-Dublin (CBL) 914; and Brit. Mus. Orient. No. 652, which is PEMM MS BOr (BL) 652.</p>"
