    "id": "197",
    "title": "The Jew of Constantinople who threw an icon of the Virgin Mary into a pit toliet and was taken to hell by a demon",
    "incipit": "ወሀሎ፡ አሐዱ፡ ብእሲ፡ አይሁዳዊ፡ በውስተ፡ ሀገረ፡ ቍስጥንጥንያ፡ ሀገረ፡ ንጉሥ፡ ወቦቱ፡ ፍቁረ፡ ብእሲ፡ ክርስቲያናዊ፡ ወያፈቅሮ፡ ፈድፋደ። ወኮነ፡ ውእቱ፡ አይሁዳዊ፡ ይጸልእ፡ ሃይማኖተ፡ ክርስቲያን፡ ወይጸርፍ፡ ላዕሌሆሙ፡ በነገረ፡ እኩይ",
    "manuscript_count": 163,
    "translations": [
      {
        "author": "Wallis Budge",
        "text": "<p>There was a certain king who dwelled by the sea. He had only one son, a goodly youth who was blessed and feared God.</p> <br> <p>This prince’s father, by reason of his great love for his son, brought teachers to instruct him in learning of every kind, as well as in how to direct his kingdom when he grew up.</p> <br> <p>His father set him in the church that was in his house, and the youth prayed continually to Our Lady Mary, whom he loved with all his heart.</p> <br> <p>Now there was a picture of Our Lady Mary in the church, and the youth loved that picture. When he had dismissed his teacher, he would stand before it and salute her.</p> <br> <p>One day, as he stood before the picture, Our Lady Mary spoke to him and said, “My dear beloved, believe in me. I have rejoiced in your work, and I love you exceedingly because you have loved me.”</p> <br> <p>When the youth heard these words from the picture, he marveled greatly and grew sorely afraid.</p> <br> <p>She said to him, “Don’t be afraid, for I hold conversations through this picture with those whom I love.”</p> <br> <p>The youth said, “As to your loving me, I have spoken to you on many days, and you have not answered me.”</p> <br> <p>She said, “Now, how much do you love me?”</p> <br> <p>He said, “I love you more than my father, my mother, and myself.”</p> <br> <p>She said, “I know that you don’t love me with sincerity. I wish you to take no wife except a woman who has my likeness and form. Be hold now, and observe me well, so that you may know what I am like.”</p> <br> <p>When the youth looked at the picture, he was unable to gaze his fill at her because of the great beauty of her form and the radiance that shone forth from her.</p> <br> <p>She said to him, “Give me your hand [and swear] that you will marry no woman except myself, for I am a jealous woman, and I am jealous about you.”</p> <br> <p>The youth rose, went to her, and he gave her his hand. She gave him her hand, which he took and kissed twice. He wept from joy.</p> <br> <p>She said, “Keep your virginity, for he who keeps his virginity becomes my brother, and no man in this world can describe his felicity. From now on I won’t hold converse with you again until I come and take you with me into the kingdom of heaven, where you shall be with me in everlasting joy.”</p> <br> <p>When his teacher came, he saw the traces of tears on the prince’s face and brought him out of the church.</p> <br> <p>When his father saw that he loved to sit in the church, and that he had no desire to dress himself like the children of kings and to make merry with them, he said:</p> <br> <p> “If I allow my son to indulge in this habit, he will become despised among the children of the Christians and kings. I wish to obtain a wife for him, so that his eyes may be opened, and so that he may become like the other children of men; for a wife improves a man in very many matters.”</p> <br> <p>From a distant part of the country, his father obtained for him a beautiful maiden who was like the light of the sun.</p> <br> <p>When he had brought this maiden to his house, all the nobles and all the horsemen gathered in the church, and the bishop came to crown them.</p> <br> <p>The young man hid himself, and they only found him with the greatest difficulty.</p> <br> <p>When they brought him, sorrowing, to his father, the king rebuked him and said, before the bishop, “My son, do you not want this woman?”</p> <br> <p>The young man said, “No, I do not.”</p> <br> <p>The father said to the bishop, “Don’t listen to him, for his behavior shames his wife.”</p> <br> <p>The young man was looking out for a hiding place, to which he might flee.</p> <br> <p>Then his father said, “Hold him fast!”</p> <br> <p>As the prince fled, he fell and broke his neck, and their joy turned to sorrow.</p> <br> <p>As they were weeping and bearing the young man along, they heard a loud, terrifying, and majestic voice.</p> <br> <p>They lifted their eyes to heaven and saw a crowd of angels coming down to the young man.</p> <br> <p>Among them was a glorious Lady of most beautiful form and appearance, from whose face shone a bright light; among those gathered there was none able to describe her.</p> <br> <p>She blessed the prince and said, “Behold, I have come to you, my dear beloved, so that I may take you with me into joy, just as I promised you. Give me your soul!”</p> <br> <p>The young man fell asleep and delivered his soul to her.</p> <br> <p>[The people] heard a voice that rejoiced, and they smelled a perfume that was sweeter than any scent in the world.</p> <br> <p>Then they saw Our Lady Mary carrying the young man’s soul, and the angels surrounding it until they reached heaven.</p> <br> <p>They sang, rejoiced, and praised God. Praise and thanksgiving also be to Our Lady Mary, the holy one, who saves those who serve her.</p> <br> <p>Note: This English translation was composed by Wallis Budge in the early 1900s but updated by the PEMM team for modern readers. We have replaced old-fashioned terms (e.g., thee, thou), stripped old-fashioned word endings (e.g., striketh), and corrected offensive language (e.g., lame, barren). However, Budge made some of these translations from a peculiar manuscript, which often had missing words and sentences, so the translations don't always do justice to the story in its original, earliest form. We hope to retranslate the story from better manuscripts in future. His translations are from what he calls MS A, which is PEMM MS B-Oslo (SCOL) 248; MS B, which is PEMM MS B-Dublin (CBL) 914; and Brit. Mus. Orient. No. 652, which is PEMM MS BOr (BL) 652.</p>"
      }
    ]
