    "title": "A merchant who distributed as alms the money that had been loaned to him by a friend after the merchant gave away all his wealth to the poor",
    "incipit": "ወነበረ፡ አሐዱ፡ ብእሲ፡ ባዕል፡ ወነጋዲ፡ ዘስሙ፡ ፊልጶስ፡ ዘሀገረ፡ ኒቆንያ፡ ወቦቱ፡ ምግባረ፡ ሠናይ፡ ወይምሕክ፡ ነዳያነ፡ በውሂበ፡ ምጽዋት፡ ብዙኅ። ወአሐተ፡ ዕለተ፡ ሐለየ፡ በልቡ፡ እንዘ፡ ይብል፡ እስመ፡ ሊተ፡ ብዙኅ፡ ንዋይ፡ ወብየ፡ ብዙኅ፡ ወርቅ፡ ወብሩር፡ ወእፈቅድ፡ አሃብ፡ ለነዳያን፡ ወለምስኪናን፡ እምቅድመ፡ እሙት",
    "manuscript_count": 138,
    "translations": [
      {
        "author": "Wallis Budge",
        "text": "<p>There was a certain woman who had made a vow that she would fast [in honor] of Our Lady Mary.</p> <br> <p>When her husband learned about her vow, he made her to stop fasting and forced her to eat.</p> <br> <p>One day she opened her box and found it full of gold.</p> <br> <p>Since she knew and was very sure that neither this gold nor anything else could be hers in the world to come, and that Our Lady Mary had given it to her instead of the reward for her vow, she was sad and wept very bitterly.</p> <br> <p>When her husband saw that she was sorrowful, he was very sorry for her in his heart, and he asked her about the matter of her sadness.</p> <br> <p>She said, “I am very sad because you have made me give up fasting to Our Lady Mary.”</p> <br> <p>He said, “Fast as you please!”</p> <br> <p>So, the woman fasted instead of eating.</p> <br> <p>Afterward, when she went to her box and opened it, she found no gold whatsoever inside.</p> <br> <p>She rejoiced greatly, for she knew that Our Lady Mary was well pleased with her, and that she had put her [treasure] [in the place] where there is no decay.</p> <br> <p>The woman told her husband all that she had seen, and he repented of what he had done and joined the fast. He served Our Lady Mary with all his might and obtained [everlasting] life through fasting.</p> <br> <p>Note: This English translation was composed by Wallis Budge in the early 1900s but updated by the PEMM team for modern readers. We have replaced old-fashioned terms (e.g., thee, thou), stripped old-fashioned word endings (e.g., striketh), and corrected offensive language (e.g., lame, barren). However, Budge made some of these translations from a peculiar manuscript, which often had missing words and sentences, so the translations don't always do justice to the story in its original, earliest form. We hope to retranslate the story from better manuscripts in future. His translations are from what he calls MS A, which is PEMM MS B-Oslo (SCOL) 248; MS B, which is PEMM MS B-Dublin (CBL) 914; and Brit. Mus. Orient. No. 652, which is PEMM MS BOr (BL) 652.</p>"
      },
      {
        "author": "Wallis Budge",
        "text": "<p>There was a certain noble lady who lived upon an island in the sea [or river]. She was one of the great ones of the town, and her family was high born. Her husband was also one of the great ones of the town.</p> <br> <p>That noble lady went to make her confession to the priest, and when the priest saw her dressed in purple and wearing ornaments of gold, silver, and jewels of great value, he said to her:</p> <br> <p>“My dear daughter, you must know that rich apparel induces pride, which destroys the soul. I will tell you that which shall be profitable for your soul, and that is: Give alms to the poor, the needy, the helpless, and the churches, and there shall be treasure for you in heaven.”</p> <br> <p>When that woman heard the priest’s words, she did as he had commanded her, giving her jewels to the poor and her clothing to the church.</p> <br> <p>When her husband saw what she had done, he was sorrowful and would not speak to her.</p> <br> <p>Now this noble lady loved Our Lady Mary with all her heart, and when the festival of Our Lady Mary arrived—that which commemorated her entrance into the Sanctuary—she said to her husband, “I wish to go to the church.”</p> <br> <p>When he heard her words, he chided her and said, “What will it profit you to go to church among all the great people of the town? They are dressed in purple garments and raiment of fine linen, and they and adorned with gold and silver ornaments. But you, poor miserable creature, have neither gold nor silver [brocade], having wasted your money in alms. Everyone who sees you in your miserable rags will curse me.”</p> <br> <p>He then swore by God, saying, “You shall not leave your house to go to the church.”</p> <br> <p>When she heard her husband’s words, she was sad and said, “How can [I let] pass this great and glorious festival, which is blessed because God listens to the prayers offered by the Christian people?”</p> <br> <p>She wept scalding tears with a sad heart. She lifted her eyes to Our Lady Mary, and she sorrowed because of the passing of the festival of the Virgin.</p> <br> <p>Then, from the immensity of her sorrow and weeping on account of being forbidden from attending the festival, she fell into a short sleep.</p> <br> <p>She beheld, as it were, a multitude of Christians, both men and women, coming down from heaven into the church.</p> <br> <p>The priest, wearing his holy and august vestments, began to recite the prayers from the beginning to the end.</p> <br> <p>The priest then gave a wax candle to everyone, but he did not give a candle to that woman.</p> <br> <p>When the priest saw that he had not given her a candle, he told the deacon to give her one like everyone else. The woman took the candle and rejoiced greatly.</p> <br> <p>When the consecration of the Eucharist came, the people embraced the hands of the priest and, according to custom, gave him the candles.</p> <br> <p>The woman wished to embrace the hands of the priest, for the light of his face was revealed, but she did not wish to give him the candle in her hand.</p> <br> <p>She approached the priest and embraced his hand. The deacon wanted to take the candle out of her hand, but she would not let it go. The deacon strengthened his grip, and the candle broke into two pieces.</p> <br> <p>When the office had ended, the priest pronounced the benediction, and the congregation dispersed.</p> <br> <p>The woman woke from her sleep, and when she saw the wax candle in her hand, she knew that her dream had been real.</p> <br> <p>She told all the people what she had seen, and she showed [them] the candle. The wax was as white as snow, and the odor of its perfume was beautiful.</p> <br> <p>This matter was heard of throughout the city, and the woman used to invite the people to give thanks to Our Lady Mary, the intercessor.</p> <br> <p>When her husband saw the wax and heard of his wife’s dream, [he believed], and they passed the rest of their lives in the service of Our Lady Mary.</p> <br> <p>Note: This English translation was composed by Wallis Budge in the early 1900s but updated by the PEMM team for modern readers. We have replaced old-fashioned terms (e.g., thee, thou), stripped old-fashioned word endings (e.g., striketh), and corrected offensive language (e.g., lame, barren). However, Budge made some of these translations from a peculiar manuscript, which often had missing words and sentences, so the translations don't always do justice to the story in its original, earliest form. We hope to retranslate the story from better manuscripts in future. His translations are from what he calls MS A, which is PEMM MS B-Oslo (SCOL) 248; MS B, which is PEMM MS B-Dublin (CBL) 914; and Brit. Mus. Orient. No. 652, which is PEMM MS BOr (BL) 652.</p>"
      }
    ]
