        "author": "Wallis Budge",
        "text": "<p>There was a certain bishop whose name was Mercurius, and his whole body was covered with leprosy.</p> <br> <p>One day he went to the Archbishop Zacharias, and when the archbishop saw him, he touched him and said gently and graciously, “My dear brother, it is not seemly for you to minister as a priest with this disease upon you, unless our Lord Jesus Christ remove it from you—for the Book calls a man with leprosy unclean” [Leviticus 13:44–45].</p> <br> <p>When Bishop Mercurius heard him, he wept and said, “My dear father, help me with your prayers.”</p> <br> <p>Then he departed and went to the church to which he had been appointed. He stood before the picture of Our Holy Lady, the Virgin Mary, the Mother of God. There he wept and made supplication to her, [praying] that she would cleanse him of his leprosy.</p> <br> <p>He continued to do this from the morning of the second day of the week [Monday] until the ninth hour of the fourth day [Wednesday], and he prayed unceasingly.</p> <br> <p>At the time of the ninth hour of the fourth day of the week he became weary. He placed his head in a position of rest, and as he was resting, he fell asleep.</p> <br> <p>He saw in a dream that the hand of the picture of Our Holy Lady, the Virgin Mary, reached out and rubbed his flesh.</p> <br> <p>He awoke immediately and found that his body had been cleansed of leprosy.</p> <br> <p>He rejoiced greatly and praised God, the Glorious and Most High God.</p> <br> <p>He also made thanksgiving to Our Lady Mary, Holy Twofold Virgin, the Mother of God, because she had graciously granted him mercy.</p> <br> <p class=“tab”>You cleansed Mercurius of his leprosy,</p> <p class=“tab”>And healed him completely of the disease that consumed him.</p> <p class=“tab”>O Mary, fill my feeble mind with yourself,</p> <p class=“tab”>So that I, just as one who looks at himself in a mirror,</p> <p class=“tab”>May not forget the admonitions of your Son, the Teacher of the Law.</p> <br> <p>Note: This English translation was composed by Wallis Budge in the early 1900s but updated by the PEMM team for modern readers. We have replaced old-fashioned terms (e.g., thee, thou), stripped old-fashioned word endings (e.g., striketh), and corrected offensive language (e.g., lame, barren). However, Budge made some of these translations from a peculiar manuscript, which often had missing words and sentences, so the translations don't always do justice to the story in its original, earliest form. We hope to retranslate the story from better manuscripts in future. His translations are from what he calls MS A, which is PEMM MS B-Oslo (SCOL) 248; MS B, which is PEMM MS B-Dublin (CBL) 914; and Brit. Mus. Orient. No. 652, which is PEMM MS BOr (BL) 652.</p>"
