    "incipit": "ወሀለወት፡ አሐቲ፡ ድንግል፡ እንተ፡ በፅዓት፡ ድንግልናሃ፡ ለማርያም፡ ወትትአመን፡ ብዙኀ፡ ወትብል፡ ኦእግዝእትየ፡ አልብየ፡ ምግባረ፡ ሠናይ፡ ወባሕቱ፡ ዕቀቢ፡ ድንግልናየ፡ ከመ፡ ኢይትኃፈር፡ በቅድሜኪ",
    "manuscript_count": 104,
    "translations": [
      {
        "author": "Rowan Williams",
        "text": "<p>The Miracles of Our Lady Mary, the Holy Twofold Virgin Theotokos</p><p>May her prayers and blessings be with us to the ages of ages. Amen.</p><p>Now there was a certain man of the tribe of Judah called Joachim, and he married a woman whose name was Anne,[1] from the priestly tribe of Levi. 3Both of them were good and kind people, and they walked in the Law of the Lord, for the Lord loved them and chose them from the beginning. 4However, his wife was barren, and they were sad and sorrowful because they were childless. So they went to the House of God every day, offering up prayer to the Lord with much weeping and with great groans that He might grant them a child. They distributed their wealth to the poor and needy, and to the House of the Lord; 5and they lived by faith, hoping that the Lord would accept their supplications and grant them a child. </p><p>6 One day, Joachim and Anne went forth and came into a small garden. 7And as they stood up to pray, they beheld doves rejoicing with their young. Looking on, they were sad and sorrowful because they had no children of their own, and due to the greatness of their sadness, their eyes became heavy, and they fell asleep. 8In her sleep, Anne saw her husband Joachim holding[2] a rod, which budded and produced fruit.[3] 9Likewise, Joachim beheld beautiful, sweet fruit in the womb of Anne, the like of which there is no comparison.[4] 10When they woke up from their sleep, they rejoiced and spoke to one another about what they had seen in their dream,[5] and they glorified the Lord. 11Then they went home, saying, ‘May the will of God be done.’ </p><p>12 At that time Anne conceived,[6] and when her pregnancy was made known to her, she made a vow to the Lord, saying, ‘The child which You have given me will be an oblation to You.’[7] 13So she continued to increase her prayers, fasting and almsgiving to the poor and needy with joy and gladness. 14And in her heart, she said, ‘How can I repay the Lord for all that He has done for me?’[8] Moreover, she abstained from all unclean foods and increased her prostrations and prayers to the Lord, that He might preserve that which He had granted her. </p><p>15 Six months later, Anne’s pregnancy was made known to all, and her kinsfolk and the kinsfolk of her husband heard of it and came to her, saying,[9] ‘What is this thing which has happened to you, after you have passed the age[10] of childbearing?’ And they touched her stomach to be certain of her pregnancy. 16Now there was a woman of the kinsfolk of Saint Anne’s mother, a certain daughter of Arsaban,[11] who had a blind eye.[12] She touched Anne’s stomach and then touched her eye in complete innocence,[13] and it was healed at once. ‘O Anne, blessings to you!’ she cried out[14] to blessed Anne, ‘For ever since the creation of the world there has been no one like you who has healed the eyes of the blind with her womb.’ 17Then her kinsfolk bowed down to her, and as the sun is more honourable than the morning star, blessed Anne was honoured above them. 18After that, news of her spread throughout all lands, and they brought all the sick and suffering to her. And when they touched her stomach, they were healed, and all of them exclaimed,[15] ‘What will take place when the one in her womb who heals the sick is born!’</p><p>19 When the children of Israel heard of this, they gathered together and said, ‘What shall we say concerning this matter? For behold, Joachim and Anne have torn the Kingdom of Israel apart; Anne has conceived and is healing the sick with her womb! Will she not now rule over us?’[16] 20Then one of them said, ‘Does this cause you to stumble? Come, let us stone them to death, even as Moses wrote.’ And they all agreed on a time to convene with one another.</p><p>21 So it came to pass at midnight that the Angel called Gabriel descended from Heaven and told Joachim and Anne, ‘Arise, O parents of noble birth!’ And he took them to Mount Lebanon. 22There, in the fourth division of the age, in the sixty-sixth generation from Adam, Mary was born. 23And on that day was fulfilled the promise of the forefathers, which was proclaimed by the Prophets in many symbolic forms. 24And the curse which was on our race was removed. Because of Eve, the gates of Paradise were closed, but because of the pure Virgin Mary, the glorious daughter of David, they have been opened for us once again. </p><p>May her prayers and blessings be with us to the ages of ages. Amen.</p>"
      },
      {
        "author": "Rowan Williams",
        "text": "<p>The Miracles of Our Lady Mary</p><p> </p><p>May her prayers and blessings be with us. Amen.</p><p>After that, Seth was born, and Mary was passed on from Adam and dwelt in Seth’s body. Next she was passed on and dwelt in the bodies of Enosh, Kenan, Mahalalel, Jared, Enoch, Methuselah, Lamech and Noah. She dwelt in their bodies, being passed on from one body to another.[1] Again she was passed on and dwelt in the bodies of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob. And Jacob beheld her when he blessed Ephrem and Manasseh. He said to his son Joseph,[2] ‘Christ shall be born of the Virgin whose name is Mary at the end of three years.’[3] Moreover, she dwelt in the bodies of the Prophets, being passed on from the body of one to that of another,[4] from Seth to David and Solomon.</p><p>Then she dwelt in the body of Joachim, and Joachim said to his wife Anne, ‘I saw the seven Heavens open, and a white bird went forth from within. She hovered above my head,[5] entered into my ear and dwelt in my inner parts.’ So they discussed this matter in secret on the thirtieth day of the month of hamle,[6] and Our Lady Mary was conceived on the seventh of Nahase, the day which God blessed.</p><p>Blessed are those who clothe the naked; those who feed the hungry;[7] those who comfort the grieving; those who set prisoners free; and those who visit the sick on this day. They shall receive a portion in the Kingdom of Heaven with the Prophets and the Apostles to the ages of ages. Amen.</p>"
      },
      {
        "author": "",
        "text": "An English translation of this story is not yet available."
      }
    ]
