    "id": "76",
    "title": "King Tiberius's Icon",
    "incipit": "ወኮነ፡ ሶበ፡ ሖረ፡ ዮሐንስ፡ ኀበ፡ ጢባርዮስ፡ ንጉሠ፡ ሀገረ፡ ሮሜ፡ በከመ፡ ይቤሎ፡ እግዚእነ",
    "manuscript_count": 85,
    "translations": [
      {
        "author": "Jeremy Brown & Dawit Muluneh with Bret Windhauser",
        "text": "<p>A miracle [performed] by Our Lady Mary, the Holy Virgin, Mother of God. May her prayer and her blessing be with her servant, [King] Lebna Dengel forever and ever, amen.</p><br><p>There was a man called Masfeyanos, the King of Romeya. His wife was named Sefengeya because she was barren and did not have any children. [And for this reason] the two of them grieved for fifteen years. </p><br><p>They were righteous people. They gave alms to the poor and needy and gave gifts to the churches. One day, Sefengeya traveled to receive the Eucharist. She entered the church where the icons of Peter and Paul and of Our Lady, Holy Virgin Mary were. Sefengeya stood in the midst of the icons and prostrated before the icon of Our Lady, Holy Virgin Mary. She prayed, “Give me a child who will please you and your Son. But if he will not please your son, shut my womb.”</p><br><p>She continued asking like this and the icon bowed down as though it were saying yes. Having received the Eucharist, she returned to her home.</p><br><p>After a few days, she conceived and bore a child. She praised God and named her child Issac. Issac means “mother of pearl”. When he was 40 days old, his parents took him to the church and his parents performed the customs of Christianity [that is baptism] for him. </p><br><p>All the people stood in the Church of Peter and Paul as the patriarch hugged Issac and carried him to stand before the altar. The patriarch blessed Issac saying “May the God of Abraham, Issac, and Jacob, who blessed our holy ancestors before, bless you.”</p><br><p>After the patriarch blessed the child, a great light shone over him that was as brilliant as the sun, moon, and stars. All those who saw and heard this marveled and praised God. They received the Eucharist and returned to their homes.</p><br><p>The queen also returned home with her baby son. Because of these events, they held a feast and rejoiced joyfully [saying], “Our dear Lady, Holy Virgin Mary, now we are consecrated as Christians. Because of you, we have joy in exchange for our grief and delight in exchange of our sadness. Because of you, we have wealth in exchange for our poverty and glorious clothes in exchange for our nakedness. Because of you, we have a crown of pearls for our head and magnificent light for our faces. You are queen in heaven and queen on earth. We have received you, O Fullness and Treasury of blessings. Your intercession makes the barren fruitful and also the fruit changes into choice possessions. </p><br><p>Because of this Isaac, who is called Garima, was blessed; he who was born by your petition to complete good works. The power of your aid increased in him openly. Because of this he overcame the severe struggle; by way of the boat of your petition he crossed the waves of the sea of the world.</p><br><p>After he reigned seven years in the love of your son, then he burned [with longing], he abandoned the kingdom and journeyed to a foreign land. Moreover he performed many miracles because he was purified by your prayer. In the morning, it was he who sowed wheat and in the evening he harvested, gathering his bundles. Then he raised it into an acacia tree and he brought another bundle to the ox and he made the ox thresh the wheat. The best bundle went down into the ground and the worst bundle remained in the acacia tree. And so that wheat was used to make the Eucharistic bread for that day. In the midst of all of this your servant, who was born by your petition, arrived to perform miracles.</p><br><p>May your prayer and your petition be with your servant, [King] Lebna Dengel forever and ever, amen.</p><br><p></p><br><p>EMML 5520 Ending</p><br><p>And after a few days, she conceived and bore a son and called him Isaac. And king Mesfeyanos and his wife Sefngeya rejoiced and performed a great feast for the poor and the needy. And they praised God and our Lady Mary who does shame those who petition her and she does not neglect those who call upon her.</p><br>"
      }
    ]
